---
description: Avoid curly braces around shell variables in copy-pasteable tutorial commands
globs: **/*.md,**/*.yml,**/*.yaml,**/*.sh
alwaysApply: false
---

# No Curly Braces on Shell Variables

This is a tutorial repo — every shell command and manifest is meant to be
copy-pasted verbatim by a reader. Some Mac apps/terminals mangle `${VAR}`
during copy-paste (inserting stray backslashes before the braces), silently
breaking variable expansion and producing confusing empty/broken output.

Use the bare `$VAR` form instead of `${VAR}` wherever the shell doesn't
require braces to disambiguate.

```bash
# BAD
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $MAAS_API_KEY" https://${ROUTE_HOST}/maas-api/v1/models

# GOOD
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $MAAS_API_KEY" https://$ROUTE_HOST/maas-api/v1/models
```

This applies to shell commands in `README.md` code blocks and to
`envsubst`-templated YAML manifests (`envsubst` supports both `$VAR` and
`${VAR}` syntax, so switching to bare `$VAR` is safe).

## When braces are still required — keep them

Only use `${VAR}` when the shell genuinely needs it:

- Parameter expansion with an operator, e.g. `${VAR:-default}`, `${VAR:?err}`
- The variable is immediately followed by an identifier character
  (letter/digit/underscore) that would otherwise merge into the name, e.g.
  `${VAR}_suffix` or `${VAR}text`

Scripts that live on disk and are executed directly (e.g. `./verify.sh`)
rather than copy-pasted are not affected by this issue and don't need to be
rewritten just for this rule — but new code in them should still prefer
`$VAR` unless one of the exceptions above applies.
